---
// End of lease and bond cleaning page, shipping at /house-cleaning/end-of-lease-cleaning/.
//
// PROVENANCE, AND WHY IT MATTERS HERE. Every other page in this repo was
// built from a supplied content brief with approved copy. This one was NOT.
// There is no end-of-lease brief. The copy below is DRAFTED from
// "TLB Cleaning - Brand Foundation v2.0.md" (the BFD), which is a brand and voice
// document, not a content brief. So:
//
//   * Voice, terminology, CTA wording, the claim rules and the compliance
//     rules all trace to a real approved source (the BFD).
//   * The OPERATIONAL SUBSTANCE does not. The inclusions list, the
//     exclusions list, the re-clean policy, the timings and the pricing
//     answers are drafted from category convention, not from TLB's own
//     scope of works. Each one is flagged [CONFIRM] at its definition.
//
// Treat this page as a structured first draft for Teagan to correct, not as
// approved copy awaiting a proofread. content-plans/end-of-lease-cleaning.md
// lists every open item in one place.
//
// THE ONE RULE THIS PAGE MUST NOT BREAK. BFD 5.3 (Australian Consumer Law)
// and 3.5 (Guarantees) both say the same thing: do NOT promise a bond back.
//
//   "Never guarantee outcomes TLB cannot control (e.g. do not promise
//    '100% bond back guaranteed' unless the business has formally adopted
//    and can honour such a guarantee; otherwise use 'cleaned to inspection
//    standard' style language)."
//
// This is the single most tempting claim on the page and the category's
// competitors all make it. Nothing below promises a bond, a pass, or a
// refund. The page says "cleaned to the standard the inspection is measured
// against" and stops there. If Teagan formally adopts a guarantee, that is a
// deliberate decision to make once and then wire through this page, the FAQ
// and the meta description together. Do not add it piecemeal.
//
// TERMINOLOGY. BFD 3.3 says pick one consumer term and stay with it rather
// than alternating "bond clean" / "exit clean" / "vacate clean". BFD 2.3
// says use "bond clean" for consumers and "end of lease clean" for agency
// audiences. This page serves BOTH audiences, so it does the one thing that
// satisfies both rules: it states once, in section 2, that the names mean
// the same job, then uses "bond clean" in consumer-facing copy and "end of
// lease clean" in the agency-facing card and FAQ.
//
// Changed 15 Sep 2026. Section 2's synonym sentence now names "vacate
// clean", "exit clean" and "move out clean" as well, because all three are
// mapped secondaries that appeared nowhere in the copy and a Queensland-side
// lease usually says vacate or exit. They are named ONCE, in the sentence
// whose whole job is to settle the vocabulary, and are never used again.
// That is still BFD 3.3 being followed rather than broken: the rule is
// against alternating between them in running copy, not against telling a
// reader which words mean the same thing.
//
// RESERVED SLOTS. Photo and video slots with no `src` render as labelled
// dashed boxes at the right size. That is the correct state for a slot with
// no honest asset yet (IMAGE-GUIDELINES.md section 6). Never fill one with
// invented photos, quotes or names. Prompts for the outstanding slots are in
// src/assets/home_cleaning/inside_your_home/end_of_lease_and_bond_cleaning/IMAGE-PROMPTS.md.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/sections/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TrustBar from '../../components/sections/TrustBar.astro';
import Backdrop from '../../components/ui/Backdrop.astro';
import TextBlock from '../../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import PathwayCards from '../../components/sections/PathwayCards.astro';
import ContentGrid from '../../components/sections/ContentGrid.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../../components/sections/ComparisonTable.astro';
import ServiceBlocks from '../../components/sections/ServiceBlocks.astro';
// Before/after. Both pairs are REAL TLB job photography.
import BeforeAfter from '../../components/sections/BeforeAfter.astro';
// Before/after pairs — real TLB job photos. The `sample-` files are flat
// illustrations and stay only where no real pair exists for that subject yet.
// The Pages CMS imports sit here, where the four before/after photo imports
// used to be, on purpose: Astro orders a page's CSS partly by import
// position, and moving them changed the order of this page's stylesheets.
import { localBusiness } from '../../data/business';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { getPage } from '../../lib/cms';
import * as section from '../../lib/cms-schemas';
import { sized, highlightColumn } from '../../lib/cms-schemas';
import VideoFeature from '../../components/sections/VideoFeature.astro';
import TagCloud from '../../components/sections/TagCloud.astro';
import GoogleReviewSlider from '../../components/sections/GoogleReviewSlider.astro';
import { googleBusinessProfileUrl, googleReviews } from '../../data/googleReviews';
import { locationGroups } from '../../data/locations';
import { guidePages } from '../../data/guides';
import type { GuideHref } from '../../data/guides';
import Faq from '../../components/sections/Faq.astro';
import CallToAction from '../../components/sections/CallToAction.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/sections/SiteFooter.astro';
import logoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-horizontal.png';
// Hero: a true cut-out figure, matching every other service page. The
// homepage's `service-end-of-lease-and-bond-cleans.png` used to sit here, but
// despite being a PNG it is a full scene photo with its own room behind it,
// so this hero was the only one on the site showing a boxed photograph where
// the siblings show a figure standing on the page's own colour. Same uniform
// as the mould page's cut-out (aqua polo, teal apron, teal gloves), per
// IMAGE-GUIDELINES.md section 3. The homepage tile keeps the scene photo.
// Section 3 pathway cards. Real TLB photos (6 Oct 2026), copied into this
// folder and pre-cropped to the cards' 4:3. Each is the same photo the site
// already uses for that situation, so one idea never looks like two: the
// cleared room is house cleaning's "I'm moving out", the window-frame wipe is
// the homepage's "I manage properties", and the shower niche is deep
// cleaning's "I'm not sure which I need".
// Section 2 backdrop behind the Cream scrim, shared with the trust bar.
import footerLogoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../../data/navigation';
import { trustBarCards } from '../../data/trust';
import { howItWorks, managedForYou, theLineBody } from '../../data/brand-lines';

// Section 0. Page metadata.
//
// The description deliberately does NOT say "bond back". It names the
// service, the area and what the page actually publishes, which is the
// strongest honest claim available. See the guarantee note in the file
// header.
//
// TITLE REVERTED TO THE WORKBOOK 16 Sep 2026, on TLB's instruction to ship
// the workbook copy verbatim. This is row 5's approved title, confirmed
// character-for-character against the workbook's own length column (46).
//
// What that instruction overrides, recorded so the trade is not lost:
//
//  1. The 15 Sep rewrite shipped "End of Lease Cleaning & Bond Cleans"
//     because it carries the exact-match primary `end of lease cleaning`
//     (4,800/mo, Keyword Mapping workbook 10 Aug 2026) contiguously and
//     picks up `bond cleaning services` (300/mo) with its second half.
//     "End of Lease Cleaning NSW & QLD" carries the primary contiguously
//     too, but drops the `bond cleaning` pickup for the jurisdictions.
//  2. It competes with the guide. /guides/what-the-law-actually-says-end-
//     of-lease-cleaning-in-nsw-and-qld/ ships as "End of Lease Cleaning
//     Requirements NSW & QLD", so a service page and a guide now sit in the
//     same SERP with near-identical titles. Watch which one Google picks;
//     if it prefers the guide for service intent, this is the first thing
//     to change back.
//
// 🚩 META SET FROM THE KEYWORD WORKBOOK 16 Sep 2026, on TLB's explicit
// instruction to ship the workbook copy verbatim. This reverses the 15 Sep
// ruling recorded here, which held that this line could NOT be published.
// The objections were not withdrawn on their merits, so they stand on the
// record and TLB has accepted them:
//
//  1. ⚠️ "Passes the first inspection" is an outcome claim. BFD 12.2
//     forbids guaranteeing an inspection result, and the guarantee note in
//     this file's header is the long version of why.
//  2. ⚠️ It contradicts this page's own FAQ 2, which says in as many words
//     that we cannot promise the agent signs off first time. A visitor who
//     clicks the SERP snippet lands on copy that takes the promise back.
//     These two cannot both be right; the body copy was left as-is because
//     the instruction was to change the meta, not the page.
//  3. ⚠️ "Fixed quote" contradicts §6 and §10, which both say condition
//     sets the price.
//
// Also note the geo: "across NSW and QLD" replaces the standing phrase
// "across the Northern Rivers and the Tweed", so this page now claims two
// whole states rather than the actual footprint. It reads wider than we are.
//
// 140 characters, so no truncation risk. If a tenant ever holds TLB to the
// first-inspection promise, this comment is the audit trail.
//
// In `pageMeta`, on `canonical`:
// Domain still a placeholder, same as every other page. Confirm before launch.

// PAGE-LOCAL CTA LABEL, added 15 Sep 2026 - the same override
// deep-cleaning.astro already carries, for the same reason and with the same
// scope.
//
// The shared `quoteCta` still reads "Get an instant quote" everywhere. On
// THIS page that contradicted the copy directly above and below it: BFD 13.2
// names bond cleans as work instant pricing works badly for, the hero lead
// says "fast quoting", and the pricing block argues at length that a bond
// clean cannot be priced sight unseen. The button was the last thing on the
// page still saying "instant".
//
// Overridden here rather than in navigation.ts because that export serves
// every page, including the genuinely instant-priceable ones. The site-wide
// split is still the Phase 2.3 / step 7 job in WEBSITE-UPDATE-PLAN-BFD-V2.md.
//
// The SITE HEADER's button keeps the shared label deliberately. It is a
// global component and changing it would relabel the regular-cleaning pages,
// where "instant" is correct per BFD 13.2. That needs a per-page label or a
// site-wide decision, not a copy edit on this file.
//
// `href` is unchanged, so it routes exactly where it did.
const pageQuoteCta = { ...quoteCta, label: 'Get a fast quote' };

// Section 1. Hero. Same shape as every other service page:
// split-single-image, image right, cut-out figure standing on the section
// edge via contain-bottom.
//
// The headline names the service AND the standard, which is the honest
// version of the promise a competitor makes with a guarantee badge.
//
// H1 gained the service term 15 Sep 2026. "Cleaned to the standard the
// inspection measures" carried none of the mapped primary; one word replaced
// puts `end of lease cleaning` at the front of the H1 verbatim and leaves the
// rhythm and the meaning of the original line intact.
//
// The lead's opening said "end of lease cleanS", a different word form, so
// the primary did not land in the opening paragraph either. It now does. The
// two-audience framing and the pricing line are untouched.
//
// In `hero`, on "A TLB cleaner scrubbing the kitchen sink beside a garden window":
// A real TLB photo since 6 Oct 2026, replacing the transparent cut-out
// (which ran with `contain-bottom`). Default `cover` fit now. The file is
// a slightly portrait crop (0.9:1) of the full-resolution original, the
// same framing as the house cleaning hero: the desktop panel is roughly
// square, and the earlier tighter crop had its sides trimmed there and
// read as zoomed in. This shows the photo's full width. `ratio` is the
// mobile fallback.

// Section 2. Definition paragraph. The answer-target block for
// `bond cleaning` and `end of lease cleaning`, and the GEO passage most
// likely to be lifted whole by an AI overview (BFD 3.6.2 asks for
// definition-style sentences and semantic triples, so the first sentence is
// a definition and the second is subject-predicate-object).
//
// The second paragraph is where the page settles its terminology once, so
// the rest of the page can use both names without reading as two services.
//
// In `definitionBody`, on its second paragraph:
// Widened 15 Sep 2026. `move out house cleaning` (80/mo), `vacate
// cleaning` and `exit clean` (both unmeasured rather than absent, Keyword
// Mapping workbook 10 Aug 2026) are mapped secondaries that appeared
// nowhere on the page. This is the one sentence on the site whose job is to
// settle the vocabulary, so it is the one place they can be named without
// the page reading as five services. See the TERMINOLOGY note in the file
// header before shortening this back.

// Section 3. Pathway cards. The page has two genuinely different readers
// (BFD 4.1 audiences 1 and 3) and they want opposite things from it: a
// tenant wants to know what it costs and whether it will pass, a property
// manager wants capacity and turnaround. Rather than write one blended page
// that serves neither, the cards split them at the top.
//
// Card 3 lands on section 8's comparison table, the same #compare pattern
// deep-cleaning.astro uses.
//
// Section heading/accent and the three CTA labels are DRAFTED, not briefed.

// Section 4. What's included in a bond clean.
//
// [CONFIRM THE WHOLE LIST WITH TEAGAN.] This is the most consequential block
// on the page and the one with the weakest provenance. It is drafted from
// what a bond clean conventionally covers in NSW and QLD, NOT from TLB's own
// scope of works, because no scope of works was supplied.
//
// It matters more than the equivalent list on the deep-cleaning page,
// because a tenant will hold TLB to it against money. If TLB's real scope
// differs on even one line (ovens, tracks, walls, garages), this page has
// promised something the team is not pricing for. Whoever quotes these jobs
// has to read it line by line before launch.
//
// SHAPE. Same as deep-cleaning section 5: ContentGrid `text` cells, not
// ServiceBlocks `icon-grid`, because ServiceIcon.astro has no room icons and
// six rooms would share three unrelated glyphs. Tasks run as one
// comma-separated sentence per room rather than bullets, matching the
// deep-cleaning page so the two lists read as one company's standard.
//
// Six cells in two columns is three clean rows, so nothing needs a span here
// (unlike deep-cleaning's orphaned fifth cell).

// Section 4b. The two exclusion groups. Callout, not two more grid cells:
// they are the opposite of everything above them, and an inset panel that
// reads as an interruption is exactly Callout's job.
//
// [CONFIRM], and this is the higher-stakes half. On a bond clean an
// unexpected exclusion is not just a complaint, it is a line item on an exit
// report and a deduction from someone's bond. The FAQ "What isn't included
// in a bond clean?" restates this list, so the two have to be settled
// together and kept in step.
//
// The carpet line is the one to check first. In both NSW and QLD a lease may
// specifically require professional carpet cleaning, so a tenant reading
// "quoted separately" needs to understand it is available and commonly
// needed, not that TLB will not do it.

// Section 5. How the standard is set. This is the section that does the work
// a bond-back guarantee would have done, honestly.
//
// The argument: a bond clean is not measured against "clean", it is measured
// against the entry condition report, allowing for fair wear and tear. That
// is genuinely useful to a tenant who has never done this before, and it is
// the reason TLB can be confident about the clean without promising an
// outcome that depends on the agent, the report and the state of the
// property before anyone arrived.
//
// LEGAL REVIEW. These three cells describe tenancy process in NSW and QLD.
// They are written generally and deliberately avoid stating any timeframe,
// dollar figure or entitlement, because those differ by state and change.
// Even so, this is the one block on the site that describes how a legal
// process works. It should be read by someone who can confirm it before
// launch, and outbound links to NSW Fair Trading and the Queensland
// Residential Tenancies Authority should be added (BFD 3.3 asks for
// authoritative external sources) once there is a guides section to host
// them.
//
// In `standardBlocks`, on the body of "It starts with the entry report":
// Middle sentence added 15 Sep 2026. "Reasonably clean" is the statutory
// phrase every tenancy resource on this SERP uses, and a searcher will
// usually have read it somewhere else before landing here. It was absent
// from this page entirely.
//
// Deliberately stated at the level of what the law asks for, with no Act,
// section, timeframe or entitlement named. The detail on the two Acts,
// the two regulators and the two tribunals stays on
// /guides/what-the-law-actually-says-end-of-lease-cleaning-in-nsw-and-qld/,
// which is where it can be dated and reviewed. Do not "improve" this by
// adding a specific without a compliance read - see the LEGAL REVIEW note
// above.

// Section 6. If the agent comes back. THE section a competitor would replace
// with a bond-back guarantee badge.
//
// [CONFIRM THE RE-CLEAN POLICY WITH TEAGAN BEFORE LAUNCH. This is the
// highest-risk unconfirmed thing on the page.] The copy below commits TLB to
// returning, within a stated window, at no charge, for items on the report
// that were in the agreed scope. That is a promise about money and time,
// drafted here from category convention because a page that says nothing
// about what happens next is worse than useless to a nervous tenant.
//
// Three things still need Teagan's answer:
//   1. The window (competitors typically offer 72 hours to 7 days).
//   2. Whether the re-clean is free for in-scope items only, which is what
//      is written, or broader.
//   3. Whether the client has to notify TLB before responding to the agent.
//
// If Teagan does not want to offer a re-clean at all, DELETE this section
// and its matching FAQ rather than softening them. A vague promise here is
// worse than no promise.
//
// BRACKET REMOVED FROM THE SECOND PARAGRAPH, 15 Sep 2026. It read
// "[CONFIRM: the re-clean window, for example 72 hours or 7 days]" and it
// was rendering to READERS, in the middle of the paragraph that carries
// TLB's whole answer to two competitors' bond-back guarantees. The
// replacement below is publishable today and invents no window.
//
// SWAP IT BACK THE MOMENT THE WINDOW IS CONFIRMED. A stated number is
// stronger than general urgency, and the sentence to restore is:
//   'Let us know within 72 hours of the inspection, so we can get back in
//    before the keys are gone and the matter moves on without us.'
// with 72 hours replaced by whatever Teagan actually confirms. The matching
// FAQ answer ("Let us know quickly, before the keys change hands") moves
// with it.

// Section 6b. What we give you for the agent. NEW, 15 Sep 2026.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS. Two reasons, and neither is padding.
//
// Search: it is the only home on the page for `end of lease cleaning company`
// (200/mo, Keyword Mapping workbook 10 Aug 2026), which had no coverage at
// all. The phrase is what somebody types when they want a business with
// paperwork behind it rather than a person with a bucket.
//
// Competitors: the 15 September audit found Jim's Cleaning leads both its NSW
// and its Byron Bay end of lease pages on full receipts and service dockets
// for the property manager. This page's FAQ 6 already tells a tenant the
// agent will want a receipt, and then never says TLB provides one. That is
// the gap this closes.
//
// It also does real work for section 6 above: the invoice is the document
// that settles what was in scope, which is how a free re-clean happens
// without an argument.
//
// CONFIRM BEFORE LAUNCH, and this is an operational claim about a document a
// client will hold TLB to:
//   1. That invoices are itemised to TASK level, not just to "bond clean".
//   2. That carpet steam cleaning is separately receipted when it is booked.
// If either is not true today it is a small change to how invoices are
// issued, not a reason to soften the copy - but the copy cannot ship ahead of
// the change.

// Section 7. Both sides of the border. A genuine local differentiator, and
// the reason this page names two states where other pages quietly drop the
// Queensland half.
//
// TLB's service area straddles the NSW/QLD border at Tweed Heads and
// Coolangatta. A tenant moving from Tweed Heads to Coolangatta changes legal
// jurisdiction while moving four kilometres, and most cleaners on either
// side work only one of them. This is exactly the "reinforce the local
// advantage" theme in BFD 4.5.
//
// The copy names the two regulators as the bodies that set the rules, which
// is factual and is the kind of authoritative reference BFD 3.3 asks for. It
// makes no claim about what either body requires. Keep it that way unless
// the claim is checked.

// Section 7b. Before you book, three things worth reading. NEW, 15 Sep 2026.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS. All three end of lease guides link TO this page and this
// page linked to none of them, so the internal linking ran one way only and
// the page passed nothing back to the three articles that support it.
//
// ANCHOR TEXT IS WRITTEN HERE rather than taken from guides.ts' shared
// `shortLabel` and `blurb`, which is the exception to this codebase's usual
// rule. Two reasons, both deliberate. The shared labels are shortened for
// pill lists ("The end of lease checklist", "What a bond clean costs") and
// dropping "cleaning" is exactly the word the link is supposed to pass. And
// each guide holds its own mapped target - `end of lease cleaning
// requirements` 150/mo, `end of lease cleaning checklist` unmeasured,
// `end of lease cleaning cost` 800/mo (workbook, 10 Aug 2026, rows 61-63) -
// so the anchors are written to hand those phrases over rather than to
// compete with them from a service page.
//
// The `href` and the icon still come from guides.ts, so a slug change stays
// a one-file fix and the GuideHref union still makes a link to a guide that
// does not exist a type error rather than a 404 a reader finds.
//
// The lead's last clause ("one of them tells you when you do not need to
// hire anybody") is the BFD 9.1 "straight with people" rule doing the same
// job pathway card 3 does: routing a reader away from the sale when the sale
// is not what they need.
const readingGuide = (href: GuideHref, title: string, description: string) => ({
  icon: guidePages.find((guide) => guide.href === href)!.icon,
  title,
  description,
  href,
});

// Section 8. The comparison table.
//
// Rows and framing are the deep-cleaning page's table with the highlight
// moved to the End of lease column, because a visitor arriving here from
// that page's card 2 should meet the same three services described the same
// way. One row is new ("Inside ovens and cupboards"), because it is the
// single clearest concrete difference between a bond clean and a deep clean
// and it is the thing people actually get wrong when they book.
//
// The copy is NOT extracted into src/data/ yet: two callers is this
// codebase's stated bar for sharing, and the highlight and column order
// legitimately differ per page. Worth extracting if a third page needs it.
//
// In `comparisonTable`, on "Who decides it passed":
// Replaced the old "How long" row per the updated brief, same as the
// deep-cleaning page. It lands harder here than there: this is the page
// where "somebody else decides" is the whole point, and the FAQ below
// still carries the duration answer the old row was hedging at.

// Section 8b. What an end of lease clean costs. NEW, 15 Sep 2026.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS. Justified by the competitor audit rather than by volume on
// this page. Raquel's Home Services publishes bond cleaning from $350 at a
// flat rate per room. Beyond Cleaning Group publishes from $50 per hour. TLB
// publishes neither a number nor a band, on a page whose only pricing answer
// is "we quote" - which is the honest answer and also the one a reader
// cannot act on.
//
// It stays SHORT and hands off on purpose. `end of lease cleaning cost` is
// 800/mo (workbook, 10 Aug 2026) and it belongs to
// /guides/how-much-does-end-of-lease-cleaning-cost/. A full pricing section
// here would compete with the guide for the same phrase and win neither.
//
// THE PRICE BAND IS NOT HERE AND IS NOT INVENTED. The rewrite brief drafted
// an opening sentence carrying "[Teagan to confirm: the price band for a two
// bedroom unit and for a four bedroom house]" and then said in as many words
// not to publish it with the bracket in. Both are right, so the section
// ships without that sentence: everything below is true today, nothing here
// is a number nobody has verified, and no bracket renders to a reader.
//
// WHEN THE BAND LANDS, it goes at the end of the first paragraph, in this
// shape and no stronger:
//   'A small unit in reasonable condition, empty, sits at the lower end. A
//    larger house that has been lived in hard, with carpets and ovens in
//    scope, sits well above it.'
// followed by the confirmed figures. Same request is open for the
// deep-cleaning page, raised 14 Sep 2026 - answer both at once.

// Section 9. Credibility signals.
//
// These frames are REAL TLB JOB PHOTOGRAPHS, published with the client's
// permission and with nothing identifying in frame — no house numbers, street
// signs, mail, people, or names on doors. That is why this section carries no
// "illustrative samples" note. Do NOT wire the `sample-` files back in without
// restoring a note that says so: a fabricated "before and after from a real
// job" is a false trust claim, not filler.
//
// ⚠️ BOTH PAIRS ARE KITCHEN SURFACES. The shower/bathroom pair this section
// used to carry was dropped rather than shown as an illustration next to a
// photograph, so nothing on this page evidences bathroom work. A real shower
// pair is the outstanding shoot; when it lands it takes slot 2 back.
//
// This is a DRAGGABLE COMPARISON SLIDER (BeforeAfter.astro), not the
// PhotoGallery grid this section used to be — the same change house-cleaning
// made and for the same reason. A grid lays four independent frames out and
// leaves the viewer to hold one in memory while looking at the next; the
// slider makes the PAIR the unit and does the comparison for them, in place.
//
// Shoot each pair from ONE FIXED POSITION. The slider wipes one frame over
// the other in place, so a shifted camera or a different crop reads as a
// glitch rather than as cleaning — a requirement the component cannot
// enforce and the brief has to.
//
// The photo brief. Both slots now carry real photography — cooktop and
// kitchen — so an oven interior AND a shower are both still outstanding, and
// nothing on this page evidences bathroom work:
//   1. [done] BEFORE. Cooktop, genuine TLB bond clean (oven interior wanted).
//   2. [done] AFTER.  The same cooktop, same angle.
//   3. [TODO] BEFORE. Shower screen and grout, genuine TLB bond clean.
//   4. [TODO] AFTER.  The same shower, same angle.
//
// Two consent conditions, both on TLB before these publish, and they bind
// harder here than on the deep-cleaning page: written permission, and no
// identifying detail in frame. A "before" photo of a rental at the end of a
// tenancy can identify both a former tenant and a managed property, and BFD
// 5.3 (Privacy) is explicit about client property images. Get the agency's
// permission as well as the tenant's where the property is managed.
//
// Shoot each pair from ONE fixed position or the pair does not read.

// Section 10. Video. Reserved 16/9 slot. `headingLines` is drafted.
//
// Closing full stop dropped 15 Sep 2026, same house-style pass as the
// BeforeAfter heading and the closing block.

// Section 11. Booking and pricing. TextBlock `dark` for the same reason as
// the deep-cleaning page: it sits between two light sections, it carries the
// page's second CTA, and it argues rather than lists.
//
// [CONFIRM] on the second paragraph. "You get a price up front" is a process
// promise. It matches the instant-quote positioning in BFD 1.5
// differentiator 5, but confirm the quoting flow actually returns a price
// for bond cleans rather than a callback, because bond cleans are
// condition-dependent in a way regular cleans are not.
//
// First paragraph added 15 Sep 2026. The section opened procedurally, on
// "Book online in a couple of minutes", which is an instruction to somebody
// who has already decided. The new opener agitates something the reader is
// actually feeling at this point in a move (BFD 5.2) and does it without
// implying their house or their organisation is the problem. The three
// paragraphs under it are good and are untouched.

// Section 13. Where we clean. The shared 56-town, three-region list from
// src/data/locations.ts. The open questions (the /locations/<slug>/ pattern,
// the ~41 town pages that do not exist yet, and the header mega-menu's
// shorter list) are flagged there, where one fix serves every page.

// Section 14. FAQs. Ten questions, one block, matching deep-cleaning.astro
// rather than the branded/non-branded split the older pages use. BFD 3.5
// asks for an FAQ block on every service page with self-contained 40 to 80
// word answers, which is what these are.
//
// EVERY ANSWER HERE IS DRAFTED, not briefed. The ones carrying operational
// numbers or commitments are flagged individually.
//
// In `faqItems`, on "What happens if the property fails the exit inspection?":
// [CONFIRM] the re-clean policy. Same fact as section 6, so the two have
// to move together. If section 6 is deleted, delete this too.
// In `faqItems`, on "Do you guarantee I will get my bond back?":
// Deliberately does NOT promise a bond. See the file header.
// In `faqItems`, on "How long does a bond clean take?":
// BRACKETS REMOVED 15 Sep 2026. This answer carried two of them
// ("[CONFIRM: half a day for a team]" and "[CONFIRM: a full day or
// more]") and both rendered to readers, mid-sentence, in the FAQ.
//
// The two duration sentences are cut rather than filled, because filling
// them would mean inventing a number that sets a client's expectation on
// the day and misprices the job. What is left is shorter and entirely
// true.
//
// RESTORE THE DURATIONS THE MOMENT THEY ARE CONFIRMED. The sentences go
// back between the first and the last, in this shape:
//   'An empty two bedroom unit in reasonable condition is usually half a
//    day for a team of two. A larger house, or one that has been lived in
//    hard, runs to a full day or more.'
// with the real figures in place of those. It needs a yes from Teagan
// rather than new thinking - two numbers and this answer is finished.
// In `faqItems`, on "What isn't included in a bond clean?":
// [CONFIRM] the exclusions. Restates section 4b. Keep the two in step or
// the page contradicts itself about what a client is paying for.
// In `faqItems`, on "Do I need the carpets professionally cleaned as well?":
// [CONFIRM] whether TLB books carpet cleaning in or refers it out. The
// answer below says TLB quotes it, which implies TLB arranges it. If
// that is not true, this becomes "we will point you to someone who does".
// In `faqItems`, on "How much does a bond clean cost?":
// Middle sentence added 15 Sep 2026, so this answer points at section 8b
// and at the cost guide rather than giving the same answer the page has
// already given once. An FAQ that repeats a section verbatim is a wasted
// slot.
// In `faqItems`, on "Do you bring your own products and equipment?":
// [CONFIRM] the product range, and the no-power scenario, which is an
// operational claim about how the team turns up equipped.

// Section 15. Closing block. Drafted. Names the standard rather than the
// outcome, consistent with the rest of the page.
// Closing full stop dropped 15 Sep 2026, for consistency: eleven of this
// page's H2s carry none. The wording itself stays - it is the best
// expression of BFD 1.5 on the page.

// CONTENT lives in src/content/pages/house-cleaning/end-of-lease-cleaning.yaml
// and is edited in Pages CMS ("End of lease cleaning", under House cleaning).
// This file owns the layout. The notes above explain why each section says
// what it says; they stay here because Pages CMS rewrites the YAML on save and
// would drop them.
//
// The reading-list links now come from the content file, so the GuideHref
// type check above cannot see them. `guideLink` below does the same job at
// build time: a link that is not one of the guides in guides.ts fails the
// build with the field named, rather than shipping a 404.
const guideLink = z.object({
  title: section.text,
  description: section.text,
  href: z
    .string()
    .refine(
      (href) => guidePages.some((guide) => guide.href === href),
      'Link to one of the guides, e.g. /guides/the-end-of-lease-cleaning-checklist/',
    ),
});

const page = await getPage(
  'house-cleaning/end-of-lease-cleaning',
  z.object({
    seo: section.seo,
    hero: section.hero,
    // Its background photo sits behind the trust bar and this block together
    // (the Backdrop below), so it cannot be left empty.
    definition: section.textBlock.extend({ backgroundImage: section.backgroundPhoto }),
    pathways: section.pathwayCards,
    inclusions: section.textGrid,
    exclusions: section.callout,
    standard: section.textGrid,
    reClean: section.textBlock,
    agentPaperwork: section.textBlock,
    border: section.textBlock,
    readingGuides: section.linkList.extend({ items: z.array(guideLink).min(1) }),
    comparison: section.comparisonTable,
    cost: section.textBlock,
    beforeAfter: section.beforeAfter,
    video: section.videoFeature,
    booking: section.textBlock,
    reviews: section.sectionHeading,
    whereWeClean: section.tagCloud,
    // Heading only: the paragraphs under it all come from Brand lines.
    brandLine: section.sectionHeading,
    faq: section.faq,
    closingCta: section.callToAction,
  }),
);

const pageMeta = {
  ...page.seo,
  // Domain still a placeholder, same as every other page. Confirm before launch.
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/house-cleaning/end-of-lease-cleaning/',
};

const business = localBusiness({
  description: pageMeta.description,
  areaServed: 'Northern Rivers NSW, the Tweed and Southern Gold Coast QLD',
  sameAs: googleBusinessProfileUrl ? [googleBusinessProfileUrl] : [],
});

const heroImage = page.hero.image && sized(page.hero.image, '1/1');

const pathwayCards = page.pathways.cards.map((card, i) => ({
  number: i + 1,
  ...card,
  image: sized(card.image, '4/3'),
}));

// Six inclusions make three clean rows. If an editor leaves an odd number,
// the last cell spans both columns rather than sitting alone, as on the deep
// cleaning page.
const inclusionBlocks = page.inclusions.items.map((item, i, all) => ({
  type: 'text' as const,
  ...item,
  ...(all.length % 2 === 1 && i === all.length - 1 ? { span: 2 } : {}),
}));
const standardBlocks = page.standard.items.map((item) => ({ type: 'text' as const, ...item }));

const readingGuides = page.readingGuides.items.map((item) =>
  readingGuide(item.href as GuideHref, item.title, item.description),
);

// The end of lease clean (the first column) is the highlighted one.
const comparisonColumns = highlightColumn(page.comparison.columns, 0);
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="right"
      kicker={page.hero.kicker}
      headingLines={page.hero.headingLines}
      subheading={page.hero.subheading}
      lead={page.hero.lead}
      cta={page.hero.cta ?? pageQuoteCta}
      image={heroImage}
    />

    {/* Trust bar + definition block share ONE photo behind a Cream scrim, so it is
        laid out across both. 90%, raised from 75% on 29 Sep 2026. Measured
        over this frame's own darkest region under the copy column, 75% gave
        4.22:1 — an AA FAIL. 90% gives 5.96:1. Backdrop.astro's `opacity` doc
        carries the method. Re-check it if the photo is swapped in Pages CMS. */}
    <Backdrop image={page.definition.backgroundImage} tint="muted" opacity={90} focus="top">
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock
        theme="muted"
        align="left"
        heading={page.definition.heading}
        body={page.definition.body}
        cta={page.definition.cta}
      />
    </Backdrop>

    <PathwayCards
      heading={page.pathways.heading}
      headingAccent={page.pathways.headingAccent}
      cards={pathwayCards}
    />

    {/* Heading rewritten 15 Sep 2026. Not one of this page's fourteen H2s
        contained the mapped primary (`end of lease cleaning`, 4,800/mo,
        Keyword Mapping workbook 10 Aug 2026). This is the section that does
        the most work on the page and the natural home for it, so the H2
        carries the phrase and "bond clean" moves into the lead rather than
        being lost. The six room headings underneath are untouched: the
        15 September audit found that list more specific than any
        competitor's in the set, and the specificity is the ranking asset. */}
    <ContentGrid
      columns={2}
      heading={page.inclusions.heading}
      lead={page.inclusions.lead}
      blocks={inclusionBlocks}
    />

    <Callout tone="note" heading={page.exclusions.heading} body={page.exclusions.body} cta={page.exclusions.cta} />

    <ContentGrid
      columns={3}
      heading={page.standard.heading}
      lead={page.standard.lead}
      blocks={standardBlocks}
    />

    <TextBlock
      theme="light"
      align="left"
      heading={page.reClean.heading}
      body={page.reClean.body}
      cta={page.reClean.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.reClean.backgroundImage}
    />

    {/* NEW 15 Sep 2026, and it sits HERE on purpose: the invoice is what
        settles what was in scope, so it belongs immediately after the
        re-clean commitment that depends on it. See the CONFIRM note above
        `agentPaperworkBody` — this cannot ship until itemised invoicing
        and separate carpet receipting are confirmed. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={page.agentPaperwork.heading}
      body={page.agentPaperwork.body}
      cta={page.agentPaperwork.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.agentPaperwork.backgroundImage}
    />

    {/* Theme flipped muted to light 15 Sep 2026. Not a copy change. The new
        block above took the Cream band, and two Cream sections in a row read
        as one section with two headings rather than as two. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="light"
      align="left"
      heading={page.border.heading}
      body={page.border.body}
      cta={page.border.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.border.backgroundImage}
    />

    {/* NEW 15 Sep 2026. The three guides link to this page; until now this
        page linked to none of them. `list` rather than cards: three rows of
        title plus one line is exactly what this variant is for, it needs no
        images that do not exist, and the guides' own pages already use it
        for the same job. Copy and the reasoning are above `readingGuides`. */}
    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="list"
      heading={page.readingGuides.heading}
      lead={page.readingGuides.lead}
      note={page.readingGuides.note}
      items={readingGuides}
    />

    {/* Wrapper carries the #compare anchor PathwayCards card 3 links to.
        ComparisonTable takes no id prop, and adding one just for an anchor
        target isn't worth a shared-component change. */}
    <div id="compare">
      <ComparisonTable
        heading={page.comparison.heading}
        lead={page.comparison.lead}
        cornerLabel={page.comparison.cornerLabel}
        columns={comparisonColumns}
        rows={page.comparison.rows}
        footnote={page.comparison.footnote}
        cta={page.comparison.cta}
      />
    </div>

    {/* NEW 15 Sep 2026. Sits between the comparison table and the before
        and afters: the table has just told the reader a bond clean is "the
        most thorough of the three and priced accordingly", which is the
        sentence that makes them want a number. Cream, so the table above and
        the slider below both keep their own ground.

        Short by design, and it carries no price band — see the note above
        `costBody` for what is missing and exactly where it goes. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={page.cost.heading}
      body={page.cost.body}
      cta={page.cost.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.cost.backgroundImage}
    />

    {/* Drag the handle to wipe between the two frames of each pair — see
        the block above `beforeAfterPairs` for why this is a slider and not
        a gallery. Both pairs are real TLB job photography and the `note`
        says so; correct it, do not delete it, if a sample ever returns. */}
    <BeforeAfter
      heading={page.beforeAfter.heading}
      lead={page.beforeAfter.lead}
      pairs={page.beforeAfter.pairs}
      note={page.beforeAfter.note}
      beforeLabel={page.beforeAfter.beforeLabel}
      afterLabel={page.beforeAfter.afterLabel}
    />

    <VideoFeature headingLines={page.video.headingLines} video={{ ...page.video.video, ratio: '16/9' }} />

    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading={page.booking.heading}
      body={page.booking.body}
      cta={page.booking.cta ?? pageQuoteCta}
      backgroundImage={page.booking.backgroundImage}
    />

    <GoogleReviewSlider heading={page.reviews.heading} reviews={googleReviews} profileUrl={googleBusinessProfileUrl} />

    <TagCloud
      heading={page.whereWeClean.heading}
      subheading={page.whereWeClean.subheading}
      tags={page.whereWeClean.tags}
      groups={locationGroups}
      note={page.whereWeClean.note}
    />

    {/* §1.3 The Line as a section heading, with §1.4 as its standfirst.
        The copy, and the reason this page gets `fast` rather than the other
        variant, both live in data/brand-lines.ts. Change it there, not here.

        `muted`, not `dark`: TagCloud above paints --color-brand and Faq below
        paints --color-surface, so Cream between them keeps the page
        alternating instead of stacking two dark bands.

        No CTA prop. §9.5 wants one primary CTA per page, repeated, and the
        closing CallToAction is directly under the FAQs. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={page.brandLine.heading}
      body={[managedForYou.oneOff, ...theLineBody.condition, ...howItWorks.general]}
    />

    <Faq heading={page.faq.heading} lead={page.faq.lead} items={page.faq.items} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={page.closingCta.kicker}
      heading={page.closingCta.heading}
      lead={page.closingCta.lead}
      cta={page.closingCta.cta ?? pageQuoteCta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
